# Application Identity Hard Bar

Jerry must never be directed into the wrong accounting product.

## Canonical identities

| Product | Canonical URL | Visual identity | Use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| New LJCOS / Cohen Entity Accounting | `https://ljcos-web.onrender.com` | Green Chairman navigation | Jerry's current accounting application |
| Legacy LJC Accounting App | `https://ljc-accounting-app.onrender.com` | Blue QuickBooks-style navigation | Legacy/reference application only |

These are different products, not interchangeable staging and production URLs.

## Non-negotiable rules

1. Before giving navigation, refresh, login, deployment, or verification
   directions, identify the product using the exact URL **and** visible product
   shell. Never infer product identity from a build number, Git SHA, deployment
   timestamp, branch name, or the word `production`/`staging` alone.
2. Never compare Git SHAs across these two applications as though the larger,
   newer, or production SHA identifies the correct app. A build is meaningful
   only inside the product and service that produced it.
3. Never direct Jerry from New LJCOS to the legacy app unless Jerry explicitly
   asks to open the legacy app. If the requested feature is missing from New
   LJCOS, report that it is missing; do not substitute the legacy product.
4. Before saying "open this app" or "do not use that app," verify all of:
   - requested product/workflow;
   - exact browser URL;
   - visible application identity;
   - whether the cited build belongs to that same service.
5. If any identity signal conflicts or cannot be verified, stop and ask one
   concise clarification question. Do not guess.
6. Directions must be task-completing steps for the verified product. Do not
   send Jerry through an environment switch merely to inspect a newer SHA.
7. Any agent that discovers an incorrect cross-app instruction must correct it
   immediately, state the correct canonical URL, and update this rule if a new
   ambiguity was found.

## Required preflight statement

Before an agent gives cross-environment directions, its internal preflight must
be able to state: `Product = <name>; URL = <exact URL>; visual shell = <identity>;
build belongs to this service = yes.` If it cannot, it must not give the
directions.

